<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chepni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Çepni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chepni are a Turkic tribe of the Oghuz family who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the 13th and 14th centuries. They raided and pressed on the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and served local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> of the Pontic hinterland. Their descendants live mainly in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giresun">Giresun</a>, Trabzon and Ordu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chepni.jpg" alt="Chepni" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Faigl.ladislav · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cepni.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chepni are counted among the 24 Oghuz Turkic tribes. In the legend of Oghuz Qaghan they appear as one of the clans of the tribe of Gök Han, together with the Pecheneg, the Bayandur and the Chowdur, within the Üç-Oklar branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>. The Dīwān Lughāt al-Turk of Mahmud al-Kashgari lists them as the 21st of 22 Oghuz tribes.</p><p>The Chepni converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and they are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, Shia or Alevi. The Turkish historian Faruk Sümer suggested that the first murids, or disciples, of Haji Bektash Veli may have been Chepni living at Suluca Kara Üyük, now a town of Nevşehir Province in Central Anatolia, and some Turkish historians claim that Haji Bektash Veli himself may have been of Chepni origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1330s groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> appeared in the coastal regions of the Pontus. Some of these nomads seem to have wandered through Trapezuntine territory as subjects of the Grand Komnenoi, and there is a recorded case of Christian Chepni. Linguistic evidence points the same way: according to the scholar Brendemoen, a group of Pontic Chepni nomads was bilingual in Turkic and Greek by the 14th century.</p><p>The earliest Turkic dialect of the Pontos was based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> dialect, shaped by the influence of Pontic Greek, and the historian Michael Meeker considers the linguistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenization">Hellenization</a> of some Turks who settled in the region not altogether improbable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giresun">Giresun</a> and the country around it were known in history as the Chepni province. Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> provinces of Giresun and Ordu, where most Chepni live, communities are found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Gaziantep</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balıkesir</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> the Chepni are a clan of the Geklen Turkmens in the west of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chepni (tribe)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The beylik held the inland valleys and parts of the coast near Unye and ruled Turkmen tribes, including the <b>Chepni</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/taceddinids">Taceddinids</a></li></ul></section>
